{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nPrivate reconciler forwarding bindings retain a second owner after the ADR-0111 private-shim policy landed on main. On the current implementation base, the old private bindings are:\n\n- `apply_inbound_records._inbound_update_write_edit_event`, which only forwards to the canonical event writer in `apply_inbound_events`.\n- `reconcile.py` module-level bindings for helpers now owned by `reconcile_helpers` / pass support / runtime: `StatusMappingError`, `preflight_status_mapping`, `_commit_binding_store_snapshot`, `_read_local_tickets`, `SelectionStaleError`, `ensure_selection_current`, `narrow_selection_inputs`, `_build_filter_target_set`, `_mutation_matches_filter`, `_build_plan_entries`, `_NoOpSyncLogger`, `_write_prev_snapshot_key_set`, `_accepts_synced_fields_out`, `_accepts_client`, `_accepts_ticket_plans`, `_advance_baselines`, `_advance_peer_parent`, `_write_facade_enabled`, `_resolve_pass_transport`, `bind_operation_runtime`, and `compose_reconciler_runtime`.\n- `Mode.rank()`, which remains only because the comparison dunders call it.\n\nThese are private internal names, so ADR 0111 requires exactly one canonical binding and atomic migration of source, test, string, dynamic import, and monkeypatch consumers.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Recensus the current base with semantic reference lookup plus raw string scans for imports, `getattr`, dynamic-load, and monkeypatch target strings for each old private name.\n2. Add a held-out RED oracle under `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler` proving the old shim surfaces are gone while observable behavior remains: inbound update writes still persist edits through `apply_inbound_events`, the reconcile spine still observes monkeypatches installed on the canonical helper/runtime owner, and `Mode` ordering keeps its current order without a public/private `rank` method.\n3. Migrate every in-scope source/test/string/dynamic/monkeypatch consumer to the canonical owner before deleting the old bindings.\n4. For `reconcile.py`, do not merely delete passive re-exports. Rewrite the in-module bare-name call sites in `_persist_and_log`, `reconcile_once`, and `_advance_baselines`-related paths so they resolve the canonical owner at call time. Verification target: a test monkeypatches the canonical `reconcile_helpers` owner and the running `reconcile.py` path observes that patch, proving there is no stale import-time alias.\n5. Rewrite `Mode` comparison dunders to compare against the existing ordering data directly, then remove `Mode.rank()`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- inbound event-write compatibility re-export in `apply_inbound_records.py`\n- canonical inbound writer in `apply_inbound_events.py`\n- reconciler pass-helper/runtime bindings and their bare-name call sites in `reconcile.py`\n- canonical helper/runtime owners in `reconcile_helpers.py`\n- `Mode.rank()` and comparison callers in `mode.py`\n- tests under `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler`\n\n## Testing\n\n- Held-out RED/GREEN oracle in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler` for canonical owner behavior, absence of old shim bindings, and mode ordering without `rank`.\n- Mutation teeth: temporarily reintroduce one removed shim/bare-name alias/rank path and confirm the held-out oracle returns RED, then restore.\n- Raw scans over the approved file impact for old private names, `getattr`, dynamic import strings, and monkeypatch strings.\n- Focused reconciler unit tests covering inbound, orchestration/reconcile spine, and mode ordering; then `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the ticket verify command set.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every old core/reconciler private name has no production, test, string, dynamic-load, or monkeypatch consumer.\n- [ ] The canonical owner is the only binding for each migrated operation.\n- [ ] `reconcile.py` bare-name helper/runtime call sites are repointed to the canonical owner at call time, and tests prove canonical-owner monkeypatches are observed by the running code path.\n- [ ] Mode ordering comparisons retain all current ordering semantics without `rank()`.\n- [ ] Focused inbound/orchestration/mode tests, raw shim-consumer scans, lint, typecheck, and the ticket verify command set pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788575745537679001,"uuid":"ad5dcfdd-55e4-43bc-a73c-67b15e325677"}